Presidential elections were held in [[Cyprus]] on 16 February 2003. The election campaign was dominated by the ongoing negotiations over the [[Annan Plan for Cyprus]]. [[Incumbent]] [[President of Cyprus|President]] [[Glafcos Clerides]] was largely in favour of the plan, while leading opposition candidate [[Tassos Papadopoulos]] wanted substantial amendments before he would accept it. Papadopoulos in the first round with 52% of the vote. Voter turnout was 91%.<ref name=NS>[[Dieter Nohlen]] & Philip Stöver (2010) ''Elections in Europe: A data handbook'', p447 {{ISBN|978-3-8329-5609-7}}</ref>

==Candidates==
On 3 January 2003 the incumbent President Glafcos Clerides said that he would be standing for re-election in the upcoming presidential election, after previously saying he would not stand again.<ref name=limited>{{cite news | first = Michael | last = Jansen | title = Cypriot President seeking 16-month limited mandate | newspaper = [[The Irish Times]] | page = 9 | date = 2003-01-04 }}</ref><ref name=seeks>{{cite news| url = https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/2625291.stm | title = Cyprus president seeks re-election | access-date = 28 May 2009 | date = 2003-01-03 | work = [[BBC Online]]}}</ref> Clerides, from the [[Democratic Rally]] party, had been elected in 1993 and 1998 and called for Cypriots to elect him for a further limited 16 months as president to give him time to try to reach a deal with [[Northern Cyprus|Turkish Northern Cyprus]] on reunifying the island.<ref name=limited/> He wanted other [[candidate]]s to unite behind him and agree to form a [[national unity government]] to try to reach a settlement; however, this was not agreed to by his opponents.<ref name=limited/>

Clerides' main opponent was expected to be [[Tassos Papadopoulos]], leader of the [[Democratic Party (Cyprus)|Democratic Party]].<ref name=split>{{cite news | title = Cyprus poll confusion may hit UN unity plan: Challenge to president by attorney-general could split ruling party in the south | newspaper = [[Financial Times]] | page = 5 | date = 2003-01-06 }}</ref> Papadopoulos was seen as being more hardline in negotiations with the Turkish Cypriots and had previously accused Clerides of selling out the interests of Greek Cypriots in negotiations.<ref name=frontrunner>{{cite news | title = Poll frontrunner sheds tough image on Cyprus: Spotlight falls on the island's peace deal in Sunday's election, write Kerin Hope and Andreas Hadjipapas | newspaper = [[Financial Times]] | page = 9 | date = 2003-02-14 }}</ref> Papadopoulos was backed by the biggest party in Cyprus, the [[communist]] [[Progressive Party of Working People]] and had been clear favourite in the election until Clerides announced he would stand again.<ref name=split/>

Clerides' campaign was hurt by the decision of his close aide and [[attorney general]] [[Alecos Markides]] to also stand in the election as an [[Independent (politician)|independent]].<ref name=split/><ref name=nervous>{{cite news | title = Nervous in Nicosia: Cyprus may never have a better chance of a deal | newspaper = [[Financial Times]] | page = 16 | date = 10 January 2003 }}</ref> Markides was standing as he believed Cyprus needed a younger, more modern, leader than the 83-year-old Clerides.<ref name=nervous/><ref name=clouds>{{cite news | first = Tabitha | last = Morgan | title = Defeat for Clerides clouds Cyprus deal | newspaper = [[The Daily Telegraph]] | page = 12 | date = 2003-02-17 }}</ref> His candidacy however was seen as likely to split the support of [[centre-right]] voters and thus assist Papadopoulos in the election.<ref name=split/>

A further seven candidates stood in the election including one, Costas Kyriacou, calling for [[free love]].<ref name=candidates>{{cite news | title = 10 candidates for Cyprus presidency | newspaper = [[The Irish Times]] | page = 13 | date = 18 January 2003 }}</ref><ref name=love>{{cite news| url = http://www.int.iol.co.za/index.php?set_id=1&click_id=29&art_id=qw1147771620203S313 | title = Poll candidate champions free love in Cyprus | access-date = 28 May 2009 | date = 16 May 2006 | newspaper = [[The Independent]]}}</ref><ref name=speed>{{cite news| url = http://www.ekathimerini.com/4dcgi/_w_articles_politics_100002_18/01/2003_25385 | title = Cypriots speed up talks | access-date = 28 May 2009 | date = 18 January 2003 | newspaper = [[Kathimerini]]}}</ref>

==Campaign==
[[Opinion poll]]s during the [[political campaign|campaign]] showed Papadopoulos with a significant lead over Clerides.<ref name=symbols>{{cite news | title = Search for national symbols to unite Cyprus | newspaper = [[Financial Times]] | page = 10 | date = 2003-01-24 }}</ref> The ongoing negotiations over the [[Annan Plan for Cyprus]] dominated the campaign with Clerides being seen as more favourable to the plan than many voters and this contributed to the more sceptical Papadopoulos pulling ahead.<ref name=flag>{{cite news | first = Jonathan | last = Steele | title = UN calls for a flag to unite Cyprus | newspaper = [[The Guardian]] | page = 17 | date = 2003-01-28 }}</ref> Papadopoulos called for the Annan plan to be amended and said that Clerides was giving away too much in negotiations.<ref name=shock>{{cite news | title = Greek Cypriot opposition leader sweeps to shock election victory | newspaper = [[The Scotsman]] | page = 11 | date = 2003-02-17 }}</ref> However Papadopoulos emphasized that he would negotiate for a deal if he was elected and ran [[Television advertisement|television adverts]] which attempted to reduce voter concerns over his previous [[nationalist]] stance.<ref name=frontrunner/>

The election campaign was low key with Clerides refusing to campaign for the election.<ref name=clouds/> On the day before the election the final polls showed Papadopoulos with a 12% lead over Clerides, with a reasonable chance of winning over 50% of the vote and avoiding the need for a second round.<ref name=veteran>{{cite news | first = Helena | last = Smith | title = Election holds key to peace in Cyprus: Defeat of veteran leader may threaten reunification moves | newspaper = [[The Guardian]] | page = 18 | date = 2003-02-15 }}</ref>

==Results==
The results saw Papadopoulos winning over 51% of the vote compared to almost 39% for Clerides, meaning that he was elected in the first round.<ref name=wins>{{cite news | first = Michael | last = Theodoulou | title = Opposition leader wins race for Greek Cypriot presidency | newspaper = [[The Times]] | page = 16 | date = 2003-02-17 }}</ref> Markides trailed in third place with 6.6% of the vote,<ref name=unexpectedly>{{cite news | title = Defeat of Clerides clouds hopes for Cyprus agreement GREEK CYPRIOT ELECTION UNEXPECTEDLY EASY VICTORY FOR PAPADOPOULOS | newspaper = [[Financial Times]] | page = 7 | date = 2003-02-17 }}</ref> while Kyriakou won 0.44% but claimed that he really won 73%.<ref name=love/> Voting was compulsory and so [[voter turnout]] was high<ref name=victorious>{{cite news | first = Michael | last = Jansen | title = Opposition victorious in Cyprus election | newspaper = [[The Irish Times]] | page = 7 | date = 2003-02-17 }}</ref> at over 90%.

The leader of Turkish North Cyprus [[Rauf Denktaş]] described the result as disappointing but Papadopoulos said he would begin talks on the [[United Nations]] plan within a few days of the election.<ref name=eager>{{cite news| url = https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/2771825.stm | title = New Cypriot leader 'eager' for talks | access-date = 2009-05-28 | date = 2003-02-18 | work = [[BBC Online]]}}</ref>

